<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qadi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as qadis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A qadi is a judge in Islamic law, responsible for applying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a> in civil, family and criminal cases. Qadis were appointed by Muslim rulers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> and Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphates</a> onwards. They served in the judicial systems of states such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a>, the Kanem-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qadi.jpg" alt="Qadi" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Institute of Knowledge Vectorization: Kaim Amin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UsulALFiqh.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
